**CBM Christoffel-Blindenmission Christian Blind Mission e.V. is seeking a consultant or consultancy team to support a humanitarian programme in Ukraine through two complementary assignments:**

1. **Humanitarian Needs Assessment**
2. **Strategic Implementation Review (mid-term review)**

Applicants may apply for **Objective 1, Objective 2, or both objectives**. CBM may contract one consultant/team for both objectives or separate consultants for each assignment. The indicative assignment period is **23 October to 2 December 2026**.

**Background**  
[Christian Blind Mission](https://www.cbm.org/), together with the National Assembly of People with Disabilities of Ukraine ([NAPD](https://naiu.org.ua/en/)) and Momentum Wheels for Humanity ([MWH](https://momentum4humanity.org/)), is currently implementing humanitarian action in **Chernihiv, Sumy, Kharkiv and Poltava** **oblasts** from May 2026 to April 2027 funded by The European Commission’s Directorate-General for European Civil Protection and Humanitarian Aid Operations. The action focuses on health, rehabilitation and protection, with particular attention to persons with disabilities and other groups facing heightened barriers to humanitarian assistance. Fore more information, please see: [Ukraine: Restoring Access to Health](https://www.cbm.org/news/news-articles/2026/ukraine-restoring-access-to-health.html)

In preparation for potential continuation and adaptation of the action under the 2027 EU Humanitarian Aid funding cycle, CBM intends to commission *two* complementary assignments:

1. **Objective 1:** Humanitarian Needs Assessment
2. **Objective 2:** Strategic implementation review of the ongoing action \[mid-term review\]

Applicants may apply for **Objective 1, Objective 2, or both**. CBM may contract one consultant/team for both objectives or separate consultants for each assignment.

The final detailed scope and methodology will be agreed with the selected consultant(s) and will take into account ongoing consultations with the European Commission.

**Objective 1: Humanitarian Needs Assessment**  
The assessment will provide an updated, independent and disability-inclusive analysis of current and emerging humanitarian needs relevant to CBM's response. It should examine:

- humanitarian needs, vulnerabilities and service gaps;
- barriers to accessing health, rehabilitation, protection, psychosocial, legal and related services;
- needs of persons with disabilities and other at-risk groups;
- availability, accessibility and functionality of relevant services and referral pathways;
- geographic areas and population groups experiencing significant unmet needs; and
- implications for the focus, targeting and possible adaptation or expansion of humanitarian programming in 2027.

**Objective 2: Strategic Implementation Review \[mid-term review\]**  
The review will analyse implementation experience from the ongoing 2026–2027 DG ECHO-funded action and identify lessons relevant to its potential continuation or adaptation.

This is ***not*** **a final evaluation**, as implementation is ongoing. The review should focus on:

- continued relevance of the intervention approach;
- implementation progress and emerging results;
- accessibility, uptake and perceived usefulness of activities;
- operational strengths, bottlenecks and feasibility;
- functioning of referral, coordination and service-delivery approaches;
- components that may be continued, adapted or strengthened; and
- practical lessons for future programming.

**Geographic and thematic scope**  
The assignment will primarily cover the current project areas in **Chernihiv, Sumy, Kharkiv and Poltava oblasts**. For the Needs Assessment, additional locations may be considered where relevant to future programming.

The main thematic areas are:

- health and rehabilitation;
- assistive technology;
- protection and psychosocial support;
- legal assistance;
- access to essential services and referral systems; and
- disability inclusion.

**Expected approach**  
Applicants should propose an appropriate and feasible methodology for the objective(s) for which they apply. This may include document review, key informant interviews, focus group discussions, community consultations, facility visits and analysis of available project and secondary data. The approach must be participatory, conflict-sensitive, disability-inclusive and consistent with safeguarding, confidentiality and Do No Harm principles.

**Required profile**

Applicants should demonstrate:

- relevant experience in humanitarian needs assessments, reviews, evaluations or similar assignments;
- experience in conflict-affected settings;
- relevant expertise in health, rehabilitation, disability inclusion, protection and/or MHPSS;
- strong qualitative and/or quantitative research skills;
- excellent analytical and English report-writing skills; and
- capacity to conduct or organise data collection in Ukrainian and Russian.

Experience in **Ukraine** and with **DG ECHO-funded programming** is a strong asset.

## BACKGROUND ON IMPACT AND REACH

IMPACT Initiatives is a humanitarian NGO, based in Geneva, Switzerland. The organisation manages several initiatives, including the REACH Initiative. The IMPACT team comprises specialists in data collection, management and analysis, GIS and remote sensing. IMPACT was launched at the initiative of Acted, an international NGO with headquarters based in Paris and presence in thirty countries. The two organizations have a strong complementarity formalized in a global partnership, which allows IMPACT to benefit from Acted’s operational support in its fields of intervention.

### We are currently looking for a Data Specialist to support our team in Syria, based in Damascus.

**Department**: REACH

**Position**: Data Specialist

**Contract duration**: 9 months (extension dependent on funding)

**Starting Date**: 1st December 2026

**Location**: Damascus, Syria.

## COUNTRY PROFILE

- After more than a decade of conflict, economic collapse, and large-scale displacement, a regime change in December 2024 marked a new phase in Syria’s history. Conditions remain volatile, marked by regular armed clashes, prolonged socioeconomic and infrastructure challenges, and limited access to basic services such as healthcare, education, water, and electricity. While it is widely acknowledged that Syria needs large investments in development in order to recover from years of destruction and economic degradation, the humanitarian response also continues at scale due to high levels of need after fifteen years of conflict, exacerbated by the economic situation and poor basic services infrastructure.
- Active in Syria since 2013, the IMPACT Syria mission has been producing key information products over the last decade to provide an evidence base for an informed humanitarian response. Activities have ranged from monthly humanitarian situation overviews and market monitoring, to displacement tracking, to supporting joint planning processes through the provision of multi-sectoral needs data, to providing evidence for durable solutions via area-based assessments.

## FUNCTIONS

The REACH Data Specialist will fulfill the following functions:

Under the line management of the IMPACT Senior Research Manager, the Data Specialist ensures the validity and strengthens the quality and efficiency of all IMPACT research cycles in Syria, in accordance with IMPACT’s standards, partner expectations, and IMPACT’s ethos of facilitating an evidence-based response. In coordination with assessment, data, and GIS teams, the Data Specialist will lead in the provision of technical inputs and outputs at all stages of relevant research cycles, including for secondary data reviews, assessment conceptualization, research design, data cleaning, data analysis, production of information products (inclusive of maps and factsheets), and dissemination of information products, etc. S/he may be involved in partner coordination, donor reporting, presentations, and input to the strategic development of IMPACT in Syria. The Data Specialist will also be responsible for developing and implementing a continuous capacity building program for data team members, and could lead on providing trainings to partners if relevant.

In his/her mission, the Data Specialist will be hosted by IMPACT’s global partner Acted and will fall under the direct responsibility and management of Acted’s Country Director and his/her delegates for all Administrative, Security, and relevant Logistics and Finance issues. S/he will therefore fully abide to Acted’s Security, Administration, and relevant HR, Logistics and Finance rules and regulations.

## RESPONSIBILITIES

The Data Specialist responsibilities include the following:

***RESEARCH PLANNING AND IMPLEMENTATION***

- In coordination with the Assessment teams, ensure that assessments are planned in line with project objectives and with IMPACT’s guidelines;
- For each assessment, ensure contribution to and review of draft ToRs with a specific focus on the sampling design, data collection methodology, analysis plan, as well as data management plan,
- In coordination with Assessment team, co-construct qualitative and quantitative data collection tools, ensuring data requirements of research cycle/assessment are met;
- Develop data processes and scripts as is relevant, ensure that support is provided by data staff in overseeing data collection, including validation as part of daily assessment checks;

***DATA MANAGEMENT, CLEANING AND ANALYSIS***

- Ensure that all collected data is stored in line with IMPACT’s Data Management Guidelines, and with the research project ToRs (Data Management Plan Annex);
- Ensure that data is cleaned, all revisions are recorded, and datasets are maintained and securely stored;
- Ensure data analysis conducted on collected data as per ToRs, and is validated by IMPACT HQ before output product drafting stage;
- Ensure the quality and accuracy of technical information provided as well as the confidentiality and protection of collected information;
- Perform additional analyses on external datasets and internal previous datasets as relevant/required;

***TEAM MANAGEMENT***

- Direct management of data team, including line management of data officers and assistants, including recruitment and staff career management, and the development of work plans and performance indicators;
- Collaborate closely with Country Representative, Senior Research Manager and Associate Research Managers, in terms of work planning and technical guidance of the relevant data staff;
- Conduct regular meetings with all data team members to assess progress in all research cycles and to review work plan;
- Reviewing data staff work throughout research cycles (cleaning datasets, analysis scripts, maps, etc.)
- Be available to provide regular support and technical backstopping.

***EXTERNAL ENGAGEMENT***

- In coordination with the IMPACT Country Representative, engage in relevant technical fora in country.
- Support the presentation of IMPACT data products to relevant third parties, to enhance their use and impact.

***STRATEGY DEVELOPMENT AND IMPLEMENTATION***

- Under the guidance of the Country Representative, contribute to the development and implementation of IMPACT’s strategy in Syria.
- Work with clusters and partner organizations and working groups to understand the various relevant data gaps within Syria and identify how IMPACT will fill these gaps;
- Engagement with HQ on research and/or coordination and provide support to relevant IMPACT Global Community of Practices (CoPs) (as relevant).

The Data Specialist will maintain the strictest confidentiality on all data collected and related processes. He/she will actively take measures to prevent the unauthorized sharing of any information and data belonging to IMPACT and its partners, or collected during his/her assignment with IMPACT.

